Q: Can not use Jquery or Javascript to Focus on an input field that has Jquery Timepicker attached to it A: <p>The base reason is that the library you are using (timepicker) has it's own `change` event handler running in background and you are trying to focus on element before it finishes its work.</p>
<p>If you closely look into your running snippet, after you are closing the alert you can see timepicker drop-down come for a fraction of milisecond and then its gone. (So we are clear that some internal event handling going on)</p>
<p>To overcome the issue just give some time to the library to complete it's background work. Use setTimeout() for that.</p>
<p>A working snippet with other enhancement (like your own trim function not required etc...)</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>$(function() {
  $('#Period1TimeFrom').timepicker({
    timeFormat: 'HH:mm',
    interval: 15,
    minTime: '08:00',
    maxTime: '20:00',
    defaultTime: '&lt;% Response.Write(Left(Trim(DefaultTimePeriod1From),5)) %&gt;',
    startTime: '08:00',
    dynamic: false,
    dropdown: true,
    scrollbar: true
  });
});

function Validate() {
  const $field = $("#Period1TimeFrom");
  const value = $field.val().trim();

  if (value !== "" &amp;&amp; value.length !== 5) {
    alert("Please enter a time in the format HH:MM");

    // timepicker steals focus – delay is required
    setTimeout(() =&gt; {
      $field.focus();
    }, 10);

    return false; // stop form submission
  }

  return true; // allow submit
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.7.1/jquery.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/timepicker/1.3.5/jquery.timepicker.min.css"&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/timepicker/1.3.5/jquery.timepicker.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;form method="post" action="/BookOnline/Booking/Manager/Availabilities/Edit/" id="UpdateForm" onsubmit="return Validate();"&gt;

  &lt;input type="text"
         name="Period1TimeFrom"
         id="Period1TimeFrom"
         style="width:85px"&gt;

  &lt;input type="submit"
         value="UPDATE THIS DAY"
         style="cursor:pointer;"&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How accurate is the == function with floats with different decimals A: <p>It looks like you're trying to &quot;solve&quot; an equation by basically just trying a bunch of different values for <code>k6</code> and <code>k7</code>.  That's not necessarily a good way of doing it, but it can work, and it's actually a lot simpler than you made it look.</p>
<p>First, let's clear up a couple of misconceptions.  You wrote:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How accurate is the <code>==</code> function</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It is very accurate.  (Actually it's an &quot;operator&quot;, not a function.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with floats with different decimals</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But this doesn't mean anything.  Floats don't <em>have</em> decimals.  (More on this in a minute.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The challenge I had was to round the values of the equation, and work with counters with decimal values.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is that a challenge someone gave you, or a challenge you set yourself?  Working with counters with &quot;decimal values&quot; is probably a decent thing to try.  But rounding the values -- that doesn't make much sense, and there are better ways.</p>
<p>So with that said, here's a drastically simplified version of your program:</p>
<pre><code>int main()
{
    float C1 = -2783.02, C2 = -3921.87, j = 2.33, W = 801.15, xs = 1.04, k6, k7, equation;

    for (k6 = -2000; k6 &lt;= -1900; k6 += 0.1)
    {
        for (k7 = -1000; k7 &lt;= 1000; k7 += 0.1)
            {
                equation = (C1/(j*j))*(sin(xs/j))/(xs) + (C2/(j*j))*(cos(xs/j))/(xs) - (W*j*j/2)/(xs) - k7/(xs);

                if(close_enough(k6, equation))
                {
                    printf(&quot;k6 = %f and k7 = %f (eq = %f)\n&quot;, k6, k7, equation);
                }
            }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This runs <code>k6</code> from -2000 to -1900 by 0.1, and <code>k7</code> from -1000 to 1000, also by 0.1.  It computes the <code>equation</code> value just like your program did, but then it just looks to see if the equation value it got is or isn't &quot;close enough&quot; to the <code>k6</code> value.  (More on this in a minute.). When I run this, I get lots of output, beginning with:</p>
<pre><code>k6 = -2000.000000 and k7 = -967.607910 (eq = -1999.995239)
k6 = -1999.900024 and k7 = -967.707886 (eq = -1999.899170)
k6 = -1999.800049 and k7 = -967.807861 (eq = -1999.802979)
k6 = -1999.700073 and k7 = -967.907837 (eq = -1999.706909)
k6 = -1999.600098 and k7 = -968.007812 (eq = -1999.610718)
k6 = -1999.500122 and k7 = -968.107788 (eq = -1999.514648)
k6 = -1999.400146 and k7 = -968.207764 (eq = -1999.418457)
k6 = -1999.300171 and k7 = -968.307739 (eq = -1999.322388)
k6 = -1999.200195 and k7 = -968.407715 (eq = -1999.226196)
k6 = -1999.100220 and k7 = -968.507690 (eq = -1999.130127)
</code></pre>
<p>This shows the, for example, when we evaluate the equation with <code>k7 = -967.6</code>, we get an equation value of <code>-1999.995239</code>, which is pretty close to one of the trial <code>k6</code> values of <code>-2000</code>.  But this isn't the only pair of values that works: as other comments and answers have asserted, and as this program shows, there are lots (basically an infinite number) of pairs of values that work.</p>
<p>And now a few last thoughts, including the things I said I'd get to in &quot;just a minute&quot;:</p>
<ul>
<li>Floating point numbers are <em>not</em> decimal fractions internally.  That's how we print them out, but it's best to think of them as being able to represent reasonably arbitrary real numbers.  (In fact, they're represented internally in binary, not decimal, which explains a lot of their seeming anomalies.)</li>
<li>Floating point numbers don't have infinite precision.  That's why , for example, the <code>k6</code> and <code>k7</code> values printed out by my program above didn't end in exactly <code>.1</code>, <code>.2</code>, <code>.3</code>, ... .  (Using type <code>double</code> instead of <code>float</code> would help.)</li>
<li>You might be wondering why I chose to run <code>k6</code> from -2000 to -1900.  The answer: I cheated; I knew that's where it would find answers.</li>
<li>Finally, here's the magic <code>close_enough</code> function called by my program above:</li>
</ul>
<p>:</p>
<pre><code>int close_enough(float a, float b)
{
    if(fabs(a - b) &lt; 0.05)
         return 1;
    else return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This function simply subtracts the two numbers being compared, takes the absolute value (<code>fabs</code>) to make the difference always positive, then asks whether the difference is less than 0.5.  (Since we're running through our <code>k6</code> and <code>k7</code> values by increments of 0.1, we can expect to find answers matching within half of that.) Please note that this is <em>not</em> a good way in general of checking for &quot;close enough&quot; equality, which is actually a surprisingly deep question.  This is an exceptionally simpleminded &quot;close enough equality&quot; function, suitable only for this rather contrived example.</p>
<p>There's much more to say about all four of these last points, but this answer is getting too long, and it's getting late.  (Maybe tomorrow. :-) )</p>
